[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-02", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 02 — the many-pack regime, and a default that measurement now supports", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-02.edn", :doc/body "# ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 02 - the many-pack regime, and a default that measurement now supports\n\n> Seed, from iteration 01's Meta: \"Blocks per query stayed under 10 across a 16x\n> sweep. The interesting regime is where a query touches many packs, and that is\n> unmeasured.\" Also from it: \"nothing in production writes one.  [WITHDRAWN 2026-09-09, later the same day] The correction that stood here was itself wrong, and the original sentence above is RIGHT. It said `net-kotobase/engine` carries the pack namespaces and sets KOTOBASE_PACK_WRITES=1 in its testnet env, and concluded pack writes were LIVE there. The first half is true and the conclusion does not follow: a source tree is not a deployment. Measured from outside with no credential -- backend.kotobase.net/_health answers 503 for three missing secrets (KOTOBASE_B2_KEY_ID, KOTOBASE_B2_APP_KEY, KOTOBA_SEED), and kotobase-cf-wasm-testnet.aozora.app/_health answers 200 with fields (canonical_cloudflare_independent, object_store) that exist only in net-kotobase/control-plane/kotobase-graph-database's worker, a tree with NO pack namespaces at all. Both trees declare an env.testnet with the same worker name, so a deploy from either replaces the other, and control-plane deployed last. So nothing in production or testnet writes or reads a pack, and these iterations are evidence for a plane that is still unwired. Root ADR-2608170300 carries the measurement.\n\n## The judge is the same judge\n\nRound trips, never wall clock. Both arms answer the same query over the same\nsnapshot and the harness exits 2 if they disagree, so a cheaper arm cannot win\nby returning less. What is added is a `packs` axis, and a second file that asks\nthe same question of a REAL corpus rather than a synthetic one.\n\n## Generate\n\n  H1  Cost is `2 x (packs opened) + (blocks read)`: two range reads open a pack\n      (header, then index) regardless of its size, and one read fetches a block.\n      Per-object is `2 x (blocks read)` (discover + fetch). So packing wins\n      while `blocks-read / packs-opened > 2`, and the crossover is exactly 2.\n  H2  One commit, one pack (ADR-2608160100) puts a query over a database with a\n      HISTORY into the losing regime, because it touches as many packs as\n      commits.\n  H3  wiki.kotobase.net's index is the losing shape: its dominant access is a\n      point lookup that reads the root and exactly one leaf. Two blocks cannot\n      amortise a pack open.\n\n## Rank - the synthetic sweep (bench/many_packs.cljk)\n\n800 subjects, 23 blocks, answer fixed at 3 rows, 6 blocks read:\n\n     packs  opened  round-trips  vs per-object\n         -       -           12          1.00x\n         1       1            8          1.50x\n         2       2           10          1.20x\n         4       3           12          1.00x   <- crossover\n         8       5           16          0.75x\n        23       6           18          0.67x   <- floor\n\n`2P + N` predicts every row exactly. The floor is 0.67x: one block per pack\ncosts three reads (header, index, block) where per-object costs two.\n\n## Rank - the real corpus (bench/hyakka_pack.cljk)\n\nThe live wiki.kotobase.net index, fetched as a CAR rather than fixtured:\n38 blocks, 13,498,794 bytes, a 4,885-byte DAG-CBOR root over raw leaves with a\n390,861-byte median. Leaf CIDs are read out of the published root, not assumed.\n\n    point lookup (root + 1 leaf, 2 blocks)\n       packs  opened  round-trips  vs per-object  bytes vs\n           -       -            4          1.00x    1.000x\n           1       1            4          1.00x    1.004x\n           2       2            6          0.67x    1.005x\n          38       2            6          0.67x    1.001x\n\n    prefix scan (root + 4 leaves, 5 blocks)\n           -       -           10          1.00x    1.000x\n           1       1            7          1.43x    1.001x\n           2       2            9          1.11x    1.001x\n           4       3           11          0.91x    1.001x\n          38       5           15          0.67x    1.001x\n\nH1 holds on real data with 390 KB blocks. H3 holds: the dominant pattern is\nparity at best and a 1.5x loss as soon as the index spans two packs.\n\nTransfer is within 0.5% at every row, which is iteration 01's `bounded-index`\nresult surviving contact with blocks two orders of magnitude larger. The\noverhead SHRINKS as packs multiply (1.004x -> 1.001x) because a smaller pack\ncarries a smaller index -- the one thing that gets better in the losing regime.\n\n## Meta\n\nThe result is a rule with a number in it:\n\n  **Pack a group only when a query is expected to read more than two of its\n  blocks. Below that, packing costs round trips and returns nothing.**\n\nThis is what makes ADR-2608160100's refusal to define a default block layout\ncorrect rather than merely cautious, and it is now measured. The same substrate\ngives opposite answers to two real workloads:\n\n  - `kotobase.net` hydration walks a novelty cons chain of width 1, and\n    `kotobase-storage-pack` records 92% of a query spent there. Batching cannot\n    help because the next CID does not exist until the previous block is\n    decoded. Many blocks, few packs -- large N/P. Packing is the ONLY thing\n    that helps.\n  - wiki.kotobase.net's index answers a point lookup in two blocks. N/P is 2 by\n    construction. Packing helps nothing.\n\nSo \"one commit, one pack\" is a good default for a write pattern that produces\nwide reads, and a bad one for an index whose reads are narrow by design. The\ndecision belongs to the workload, which is what \"no default\" means.\n\n### What this does NOT say\n\n  - Nothing in production writes a pack, so this remains a measurement about a\n    choice rather than about a deployment. Unchanged from iteration 01.\n  - The range server is in-memory. Round trips are transport-independent; the\n    byte figures are not a network measurement.\n  - The catalog (CID -> which pack) is an in-memory map in both benches. A real\n    one is a datom-plane lookup, so the packed arm is an OPTIMISTIC bound: a\n    deployment pays at least one more request the first time. Saying so is\n    cheaper than discovering it.\n  - Only the `item` index is exercised. `search`, `edge` and `year` have\n    different leaf counts and may sit on the other side of the crossover.\n\n## Seeds for iteration 03\n\n  - Measure the crossover against a WRITE policy rather than a shard count:\n    build several real commits and pack each, instead of splitting one snapshot.\n    That is the shape ADR-2608160100 actually proposes and neither bench tests.\n  - The catalog lookup is unpriced. Put it on the datom plane and count it.\n  - hyakka's dominant pattern says do not pack that index. The chain-walk case\n    says do. Neither is deployed. The next thing worth building is the one whose\n    N/P is measured to be large, and that is the hydration path.\n"}]
